---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import LinkCards from "../../components/docs/LinkCards.astro";
import { docHref } from "../../data/docs";

const sections = [
  { id: "files", title: "Instruction files" },
  { id: "loading", title: "How each agent loads them" },
  { id: "edit", title: "Edit them in OpenOrc" },
  { id: "brief", title: "The brief OpenOrc adds" },
  { id: "delivery", title: "How the brief reaches the agent" },
  { id: "next-steps", title: "Next steps" },
];

const brief = `You are in the thread "<title>" for project <project>, working directly in the project folder at <path> on <default branch>.

Tasks are records of work within this conversation. task_create saves a document without launching an agent. When asked to start a task, call task_start and implement it here in the current thread and workspace. Do not forward work to a separate task agent. Use task_update to record progress and completion. Only open a new thread when the user requests a separate conversation.
This thread is in Act mode: carry out the action the user intends. Act grants permission to act; it does not turn every request into implementation. Creating a task, editing its description, investigating, planning, and implementing are different actions. Choose from the user's request and conversation context.
Intent examples: 'Create a task in the backlog for X' means write and save the task with task_create execution=backlog, then confirm it was saved. Do not implement X or ask to start it. 'Build X' or 'Start this task' requests implementation; work directly in this conversation. 'How would we build X?' requests an explanation or plan, even in Act mode. Never cite Act mode as a reason to start work the user only asked to capture. A bare 'create a task' also saves to backlog unless the surrounding request clearly includes implementation.
Always call task_list before task_create. Never create a task that already exists; use task_update to refine it. Prefer one well-specified task over several vague ones.
Create a task only when the user asks for one, to save, add, or capture work as a task. Never create tasks on your own for follow-ups, ideas, or problems you notice; mention them in your reply and let the user decide. When asked, call task_create with execution=backlog. Write a concise title and a polished Markdown spec with Goal, Scope, an Acceptance criteria checklist, and Verification where relevant. task_create only saves the document. task_start marks an existing task in progress in Act mode; continue implementing it yourself in this thread. Manage OpenOrc tasks through these MCP tools, not Computer Use, browser automation, shell scripts, or database writes.
Delegated task commentary and permission waits stream in the thread's task card. When the user asks for progress, call task_get and report its activity: latestMessage, lastAction, waitingFor, and state. A waiting task is paused for input or permission; say that clearly and direct the user to its request. Do not equate in_progress with active implementation or claim there is no update without reading activity.

Tasks in this thread:
- <id> | <title> | <status> | <branch>

Other threads of this project are reachable with thread_list, thread_read, and thread_send. Message a thread only when its work depends on yours or you need something it knows; keep the message short and specific, and don't reply just to acknowledge. When the user asks you to hand work to another agent or have something done alongside yours, start a thread with thread_start; ask first only if it's unclear what to start or with which agent.

The openorc MCP server also offers memory_search, task_context, and memory_record for this project's history.`;
---

<DocsLayout slug="instructions" sections={sections}>
  <section aria-labelledby="files">
    <h2 id="files">Instruction files</h2>
    <p>
      An instruction file is a Markdown file of standing directions for agents, such as how to build and test the project and which conventions its code follows. <code>AGENTS.md</code> is the shared name that Codex and OpenCode
      read, and <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is Claude Code's own.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each agent CLI finds and reads these files itself when its session starts. OpenOrc doesn't change which files an agent loads, with one exception: in a conversation that reviews a pull request, Claude Code skips the
      instruction files in the pull request's code and reads only your personal one. OpenOrc shows you the main files beside the conversation and lets you edit them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="loading">
    <h2 id="loading">How each agent loads them</h2>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Agent</th>
            <th scope="col">Project files</th>
            <th scope="col">Personal file</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Claude Code</td>
            <td>
              <code>CLAUDE.md</code> (or <code>.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>) and <code>CLAUDE.local.md</code>, in the working folder and every folder above it. From version 2.1.277, it reads <code>AGENTS.md</code> instead when
              none of those exist there.
            </td>
            <td><code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, or <code>CLAUDE.md</code> in <code>CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Codex</td>
            <td>One <code>AGENTS.md</code> (or <code>AGENTS.override.md</code>) per folder, from the project root down to the working folder, up to 32 KiB in total by default.</td>
            <td><code>~/.codex/AGENTS.md</code>, or the same file in <code>CODEX_HOME</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>OpenCode</td>
            <td>The first <code>AGENTS.md</code> it finds, looking up from the working folder. Without one, it reads <code>CLAUDE.md</code>.</td>
            <td><code>~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md</code>, falling back to <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Claude Code also reads a subfolder's <code>CLAUDE.md</code> when it opens files there, and it expands imports: a line such as <code>@docs/testing.md</code> pulls that file in, relative to the file that contains it, up to four
      hops deep. OpenCode doesn't follow <code>@</code> references. List extra files under <code>instructions</code> in <code>opencode.json</code> instead.
    </p>
    <p>
      These are the agents' own rules, which can change between versions. Each agent documents them: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory">Claude Code</a>, <a
        href="https://developers.openai.com/codex/guides/agents-md">Codex</a
      >, and <a href="https://opencode.ai/docs/rules/">OpenCode</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="edit">
    <h2 id="edit">Edit them in OpenOrc</h2>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Open a conversation and press <kbd>⌘J</kbd> to show the side panel.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>+</strong> next to the panel's tabs and choose <strong>Instructions</strong>.</li>
      <li>Pick a file at the top of the tab, edit it, and click <strong>Save</strong> or press <kbd>⌘S</kbd>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The tab lists the files the conversation's current agent reads in the folder it works in:</p>
    <dl class="docs-terms">
      <div>
        <dt><strong>AGENTS.md</strong></dt>
        <dd>The folder's shared file. It comes first.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt><strong>CLAUDE.md</strong></dt>
        <dd>The folder's Claude Code file. It appears only when the conversation uses Claude Code.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt><strong>Personal</strong></dt>
        <dd>The agent's personal file from the table above, which applies to every project.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      In a thread with its own worktree, the folder is the worktree, so you edit that branch's copy. A missing file shows a placeholder such as <strong>No AGENTS.md yet</strong>, and saving creates it. When one file links to
      another, such as a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> that points to <code>AGENTS.md</code>, the tab lists it once. Text you haven't saved is kept if you leave the conversation.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the file changes on disk while you edit it, for example because the agent edited it, OpenOrc won't overwrite it. <strong>Keep mine</strong> keeps your text so you can save it over the new version, and <strong
        >Use new version</strong
      > drops your edits.
    </p>
    <p>An agent reads these files when its session starts, so a change you save applies from the next time the conversation's agent starts.</p>
    <Callout>
      The tab shows only these files. It doesn't list instruction files in parent folders or subfolders, <code>.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>CLAUDE.local.md</code>, or files pulled in through imports, which the agent may
      load as well. It can't edit files over 2 MB or files that aren't UTF-8 text.
    </Callout>
    <p>You can also copy a project memory into <code>CLAUDE.md</code> or <code>AGENTS.md</code> from the <a href={docHref("memory") + "#review"}>Memory screen</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="brief">
    <h2 id="brief">The brief OpenOrc adds</h2>
    <p>OpenOrc gives every run a brief of its own, on top of the agent's instruction files. It doesn't appear in the transcript. For a conversation, the brief covers:</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Part</th>
            <th scope="col">What it tells the agent</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Location</td>
            <td>The thread, the project, and where it works: the project folder and its default branch, or the thread's own worktree and branch. A Workspace conversation is told its folder need not be a repository.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Tasks</td>
            <td>
              <code>task_create</code> only saves a task, and <code>task_start</code> marks one in progress so this thread builds it. Call <code>task_list</code> before creating one, to avoid duplicates. The brief also lists the
              thread's own tasks.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Mode</td>
            <td>In Act, carry out what the user asks for, and tell saving a task apart from building it. In Plan, change no files and run nothing that changes state.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Other threads</td>
            <td>
              <code>thread_list</code>, <code>thread_read</code>, and <code>thread_send</code> reach the project's other threads. <code>thread_start</code> starts a helper thread when the user asks to hand work off or have
              something done alongside. See <a href={docHref("messaging")}>Threads working together</a>.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Memory</td>
            <td>
              <code>memory_search</code>, <code>task_context</code>, and <code>memory_record</code>, or a note that memory is off. When memory is on, the <a href={docHref("memory") + "#later-runs"}>project memory section</a> follows.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Here is the full text for a thread working in the project folder in Act mode. Text in angle brackets is filled in for each thread:</p>
    <pre><code set:text={brief} /></pre>
    <p>The task list appears only when the thread has tasks, and the last line changes when memory is off. Other text joins the brief when it applies:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>An Orcling's profile, instructions, and memory come first when an Orcling works in the conversation.</li>
      <li>Instructions for the <a href={docHref("browser")}>Preview</a> come before the brief.</li>
      <li>Review instructions follow the first line in a conversation that reviews a pull request.</li>
      <li>
        A recap of the conversation, with your last few requests and the previous agent's last reply, follows the brief when a conversation with earlier turns starts a fresh session. That happens, for example, when you switch to
        an agent that hasn't worked in it yet.
      </li>
      <li>In Plan mode, Claude Code and OpenCode get a note on how to hand over their plan.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Team assignments get a brief written for their task and the team's rules. A model answering a task discussion gets a short note that the discussion is read-only.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="delivery">
    <h2 id="delivery">How the brief reaches the agent</h2>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Agent</th>
            <th scope="col">Where the brief goes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Claude Code</td>
            <td>Appended to Claude Code's system prompt when its process starts</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Codex</td>
            <td>Sent as developer instructions when the session starts or resumes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>OpenCode</td>
            <td>Placed before your first message to each new process</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>OpenOrc builds the brief when the agent's process starts, and it stays the same until that process closes. The agent's own system prompt and instruction files come with it.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-steps">
    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <LinkCards cards={[{ slug: "memory" }, { slug: "skills" }, { slug: "messaging" }]} columns={3} />
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
